## [Resolver DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall.md)

- [How Resolver DNS Firewall works](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-overview.md): With Resolver DNS Firewall, you can control access to sites and block DNS-level threats for DNS queries going out from your VPC through the Route 53 VPC Resolver.
- [Region availability for DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-availability.md): Lists Route 53 VPC Resolver availability in AWS Regions.
- [Getting started with Resolver DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-getting-started.md): The DNS Firewall console includes a wizard that guides you through the following steps for getting started with DNS Firewall:

### [DNS Firewall rule groups and rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-groups.md)

This section describes the settings that you can configure for your DNS Firewall rule groups and rules, to define the DNS Firewall behavior for your VPCs.

- [Rule group settings in DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-settings.md): When you create or edit a DNS Firewall rule group, you specify the following values:
- [Rule settings in DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-settings.md): When you create or edit a rule in a DNS Firewall rule group, you specify the following values:
- [Rule actions in DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-actions.md): When DNS Firewall finds a match between a DNS query and a domain specification in a rule, it applies the action that's specified in the rule to the query.

### [Managing rule groups and rules in DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-managing.md)

To manage rule groups and rules in the console, follow the guidance in this section.

- [Creating a rule group and rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-adding.md): To create a rule group and add rules to it, follow the steps in this procedure.
- [Viewing and updating a rule group and rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-editing.md): Use the following procedure to view the rule groups and the rules assigned to them.
- [Deleting a rule group](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-deleting.md): To delete a rule group, perform the following procedure.

### [DNS Firewall Foundational Rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-domain-lists.md)

DNS Firewall Foundational rules provide you with the essential DNS protections to help you get started with DNS Firewall, with two main types of foundational rules:

- [Managed Domain Lists](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-managed-domain-lists.md): Protect your resources using domain lists that AWS manages for you.
- [Managing your own domain lists](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-user-managed-domain-lists.md): Protect your resources with your own domain lists, which are collections of domain specifications that you can reuse in multiple firewall rule group rules.

### [DNS Firewall Advanced Rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-advanced.md)

DNS Firewall Advanced Rule tier provides you with protections to help you detect and monitor for more advanced DNS threats (for example, Domain Generation Algorithms (DGA) or DNS Tunneling) or with more granular protections based on threat and web-content (for example, gambling, social networking).

- [Advanced DNS Protections](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-advanced-protections.md): DNS Firewall Advanced detects suspicious DNS queries based on known threat signatures in DNS queries.
- [Advanced Managed Domain Lists](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-advanced-managed-domain-lists.md): Advanced Managed Domain Lists contain domain names that are associated with malicious activity or other not-safe-for-work domains.

### [Partner managed DNS threat protection with Palo Alto Networks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw.md)

This topic covers how to subscribe to, configure, and manage Palo Alto Networks (PANW) DNS threat protection rules within Amazon Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule groups.

- [Subscribe to Palo Alto Networks Advanced DNS Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-subscribe.md): You can subscribe to Palo Alto Networks Advanced DNS Security directly from the DNS Firewall console during the rule creation flow, or from AWS Marketplace.
- [Unsubscribe from Palo Alto Networks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-unsubscribe.md)
- [Adding Palo Alto Networks rules to DNS Firewall rule groups](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-adding-rules.md): After you subscribe, you can create one or more rules using Palo Alto Networks threat feeds within any DNS Firewall rule group.
- [View managed rules from Palo Alto Networks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-view-rules.md): You can view the available PANW managed rule groups and their current state using the console, API, or CLI.
- [Rule states for Palo Alto Networks rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-rule-states.md): PANW rules in DNS Firewall can have different sync states that indicate their status.
- [Best practices for Palo Alto Networks partner managed rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-partner-panw-best-practices.md): We recommend the following best practices when using Palo Alto Networks partner managed DNS threat protection:
- [Configuring query logging for DNS Firewall](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/firewall-resolver-query-logs-configuring.md): You can evaluate your DNS Firewall rules by using Amazon CloudWatch metrics and the Resolver query logs.
- [Sharing rule groups between accounts](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-rule-group-sharing.md): You can share DNS Firewall rule groups between AWS accounts.

### [Enabling DNS Firewall protections for your VPC](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-vpc-protections.md)

You enable DNS Firewall protections for your VPC by associating one or more rule groups with the VPC.

- [Managing associations between your VPC and firewall rule groups](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-vpc-associating-rule-group.md)
- [DNS Firewall VPC configuration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/resolver-dns-firewall-vpc-configuration.md): The DNS Firewall configuration for your VPC determines whether Route 53 VPC Resolver allows queries through or blocks them during failures, for example when DNS Firewall is impaired, unresponsive, or not available in the zone.


## [What are Amazon Route 53 Profiles?](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profiles.md)

- [Using Profiles](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profile-high-level-steps.md): Overview of the process to implement Route 53 Profiles in your VPCs.

### [Create a Profile](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profile-create.md)

Create a new Route 53 Profile using the console or CLI.

- [Associate DNS Firewall rule groups](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profile-associate-dns-firewall.md): Associate DNS Firewall rule groups to a Profile for domain filtering.
- [Associate private hosted zones](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profile-associate-private-hz.md): Associate private hosted zones to a Profile for DNS resolution.
