- [Creating a subdomain that uses Amazon Route 53 as the DNS service without migrating the parent domain](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/CreatingNewSubdomain.md): Create a subdomain that uses Route 53 as the DNS service without migrating the parent domain from another DNS service.
- [Migrating DNS service for a subdomain to Amazon Route 53 without migrating the parent domain](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/MigratingSubdomain.md): Migrating DNS service for an existing subdomain to Route 53 without migrating the parent domain.
- [Transitioning to latency-based routing in Amazon Route 53](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/TutorialTransitionToLBR.md): With latency-based routing, Amazon Route 53 can direct your users to the lowest-latency AWS endpoint available.
- [Adding another Region to your latency-based routing in Amazon Route 53](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/TutorialAddingLBRRegion.md): If you're using latency based routing and you want to add an instance in a new region, you can gradually shift traffic to the new region in the same way that you gradually shifted traffic to latency-based routing in .
- [Using latency and weighted records in Amazon Route 53 to route traffic to multiple Amazon EC2 instances in a Region](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/TutorialLBRMultipleEC2InRegion.md): If your application is running on Amazon EC2 instances in two or more Amazon EC2 regions, and if you have more than one Amazon EC2 instance in one or more regions, you can use latency-based routing to route traffic to the correct region and then use weighted records to route traffic to instances within the Region based on weights that you specify.
- [Managing over 100 weighted records in Amazon Route 53](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/TutorialManagingOver100WRR.md): Amazon Route 53 lets you configure weighted records.
- [Weighting fault-tolerant multi-record answers in Amazon Route 53](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/TutorialWeightedFTMR.md)


## [Best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices.md)

- [Best practices for Amazon Route 53 DNS](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-dns.md): Follow these best practices to get the best results when using the Amazon Route 53 DNS service.

### [Best practices for VPC Resolver](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver.md)

This section provides best practices for optimizing Route 53 VPC Resolver, covering the following topics:

- [Avoid loop configurations with Resolver endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver-endpoints.md): Prevent DNS query loops by avoiding configurations where the same VPC is associated with both a Resolver rule and its inbound endpoint.
- [Resolver endpoint scaling](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver-endpoint-scaling.md): Resolver endpoint security groups use connection tracking to gather information about traffic to and from the endpoints.
- [High availability for Resolver endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver-endpoint-high-availability.md): Configure VPC Resolver endpoints across multiple Availability Zones with redundant network interfaces to ensure high availability and handle traffic surges.
- [DNS zone walking](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver-zone-walking.md): Understand DNS zone walking attacks and how Route 53 VPC Resolver automatically throttles suspicious traffic patterns to protect your endpoints.
- [Subnet compatibility for Resolver endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-resolver-subnet-compatibility.md): Avoid using Outposts subnets with Local Network Interface (LNI) enabled for VPC Resolver endpoints to prevent endpoint failures.
- [Best practices for Amazon Route 53 health checks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-healthchecks.md): Effective health check configuration is essential for maintaining a highly available and resilient infrastructure.
- [Best practices for Amazon Route 53 domain registration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/best-practices-domain-registration.md): Follow these best practices to avoid account and domain recovery issues when you register domains with Amazon Route 53.


## [Making API Requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests.md)

### [Making API Requests for Hosted Zones, Resource Record Sets, and Health Checks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rest.md)

Describes how to make REST requests to the Route 53 control API, which you use to create and manage your hosted zones, resource record sets, health checks, and tags.

- [REST Requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rest-overview.md): Describes the structure of a Route 53 REST request.
- [REST Responses](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rest-responses.md): Describes the REST responses that return special information specific to Route 53.

### [Making API Requests for Domain Registration and for Route 53 Resolver](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rpc.md)

Describes how to make requests to the Amazon Route 53 control API to manage domain registration and Route 53 Resolver.

- [RPC Requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rpc-overview.md): Describes the structure of a Route 53 RPC request.
- [RPC Responses](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-rpc-responses.md): Describes the RPC responses that return special information specific to Route 53.
- [Signing Amazon Route 53 API Requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-requests-authentication.md): Requests must be signed using an access key ID and a secret access key.
- [Amazon Route 53 API actions by function](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/api-operations-by-function.md): This topic lists all Route 53 and Route 53 Resolver API actions in groups by the function they perform.
- [API throttling](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/throttling-api-requests.md): Describes how Amazon Route 53 throttles API requests using request rate and change throughput limits, including token bucket sizes, refill rates, and best practices.


